WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.100
Start with two agencies holding
the same Microsoft 365 license.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.838
One runs a commercial tenant.

3
00:00:06.838 --> 00:00:10.380
Its admin center shows
per-location network assessments,

4
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.176
informed network routing to its SD-WAN,
and an Adoption Score for its workforce.

5
00:00:15.176 --> 00:00:18.587
The other runs a GCC tenant
and sees none of them,

6
00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:22.962
because Microsoft documents
each one as unavailable there.

7
00:00:22.962 --> 00:00:25.554
Nothing in the license,
the price list or the

8
00:00:25.554 --> 00:00:28.937
commercial documentation
tells the second agency that.

9
00:00:28.937 --> 00:00:33.679
The book's explanation is that three
separate decisions, made by three different

10
00:00:33.679 --> 00:00:37.713
parties, have to line up before a
feature is usable in a federal system.

11
00:00:37.713 --> 00:00:39.587
First, the certification.

12
00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:44.390
FedRAMP certifies a specific cloud
service offering at a certification class,

13
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.937
and the FedRAMP Marketplace is the record.

14
00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:50.541
A feature outside the certified
offering isn't covered by it,

15
00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:52.126
whatever the portal shows.

16
00:00:52.126 --> 00:00:54.000
Second, the environment.

17
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.528
Microsoft runs GCC, GCC High and DoD with
features that differ from commercial,

18
00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:04.800
and it publishes those differences one
feature at a time, on separate pages.

19
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.150
There's no single machine-readable list.

20
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.987
Third, the agency's own authorization.

21
00:01:09.987 --> 00:01:14.405
The authorizing official accepts the
risk of the agency's specific use:

22
00:01:14.405 --> 00:01:18.075
the configuration selected
and the integrations enabled.

23
00:01:18.075 --> 00:01:20.931
FedRAMP says an agency
should never create a

24
00:01:20.931 --> 00:01:24.637
standalone authorization to
operate for a cloud service.

25
00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:28.113
It authorizes its own system,
which uses the service.

26
00:01:28.113 --> 00:01:31.400
What you can actually use is
the overlap of all three.

27
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.100
So the first decision is simple and cheap.

28
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.108
Before the next architecture
review or authorization milestone,

29
00:01:38.108 --> 00:01:43.063
require every cloud feature a design
depends on to be listed with its Marketplace

30
00:01:43.063 --> 00:01:47.362
package, its availability in your
environment, and the page that says so.

31
00:01:47.362 --> 00:01:52.112
Workload owners declare the dependencies,
and the system owner checks each one.

32
00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:56.280
A feature with no citation is
unverified, not available.
